## Design decision: confidence comes from FSRS only

**All "confidence" values are computed from the FSRS algorithm — no custom mastery scores, no separate accuracy counters.**

Each `review_items` row stores the FSRS card state (`stability`, `difficulty`, `elapsed_days`, `state`, `due`, …). At any point in time, **retrievability** is derived:

```
R(item, now) = FSRS.ForgettingCurve(elapsed_days, stability)
             = (1 + factor * elapsed_days / stability) ^ decay   // FSRS-4 style
             = exp(ln(0.9) * elapsed_days / stability)           // common approximation
```

Use the same function from `go-fsrs/v3` that drives scheduling (already wrapped in [`fsrsx`](back/internal/fsrsx/fsrsx.go)).

| Use case | FSRS-derived metric |
|----------|---------------------|
| Item confidence (word or grammar topic) | `R(item, now)` |
| Task modality (MCQ vs free recall) | `R(item, now) >= 0.50` |
| Vocab level progress | `mastered_count(L) / CURRICULUM_SIZE(L)` where mastered = R ≥ 0.90 |
| Grammar level readiness | avg R ≥ 0.35 + all topics seen once | Lower promotion bar than vocab |
| Grammar slot selection | P(topic) ∝ (1 − R) among **all** curriculum topics | Always in rotation, not gated out |
| P(new item) | `headroom × confidence_gate(avg_R)` — dynamic, FSRS-driven |
| Quiz item priority (due first) | `due <= now` from same FSRS card |

**What FSRS confidence is NOT:** answer speed, raw correctness rate, or a user-visible "I know this" flag. Those may feed FSRS **ratings** on submit (as today), but confidence displayed and used for planning is always `R`.

