## Vocabulary level progression

### Research summary (what the literature says)

**CEFR does not define official vocabulary counts.** The framework describes communicative ability, not a fixed word list. All numbers below are research estimates, not CEFR mandates.

#### Empirical vocabulary size by CEFR level (Milton & Alexiou 2009)

Receptive vocabulary of learners who pass level-aligned exams ([source](https://ikindalikelanguages.com/blog/common-european-languages-framework-cefrand-vocabulary-size/)):

| CEFR | English (receptive lemmas, cumulative) |
|------|----------------------------------------|
| A1 | < 1,500 |
| A2 | 1,500 – 2,500 |
| B1 | 2,750 – 3,250 |
| B2 | 3,250 – 3,750 |
| C1 | 3,750 – 4,500 |
| C2 | 4,500 – 5,000 |

Important: these are **cumulative totals** measured in learners who already passed exams — not incremental curriculum sizes. Individual variation is large; no sharp threshold exists (Schmitt et al. 2011).

#### Council of Europe curriculum lists (Van Ek & Trim)

Historical specifications that influenced CEFR-aligned teaching:

| Spec | Maps to | Approx. word list size |
|------|---------|------------------------|
| Waystage 1990 | ~A2 | ~1,000 words |
| Threshold Level 1990 | ~B1 | ~2,000 words |

#### Frequency lists and text coverage (Nation 2006, NGSL)

Vocabulary size vs. text coverage (English word **families**):

| Words known | Text coverage | Implication |
|-------------|---------------|-------------|
| 1,000 | ~78–81% | Hard to read; many unknowns |
| 2,000 | ~87% | Still challenging |
| 3,000 | ~90% | Minimum for learning from context |
| 5,000 | ~95% | Adequate comprehension (Laufer 1989) |
| 8,000–9,000 | ~98% | Comfortable independent reading (Hu & Nation 2000) |

[NGSL](https://www.newgeneralservicelist.org/) (~2,809 lemmas) gives ~92% coverage of general English. Often split into **5 bands of ~560 words** for staged teaching. First 1,000 NGSL words alone cover ~98% on some constrained exams.

#### Comprehensible input thresholds (i+1)

For input to be learnable without overwhelming the learner:

| Known-word % in text | Effect |
|---------------------|--------|
| < 90% | Comprehension drops sharply; acquisition stalls |
| 95% | Adequate comprehension (Laufer 1989) — ~1 unknown per 20 words |
| 98% | Comfortable unassisted reading (Hu & Nation 2000, Schmitt 2011) |

**Design implication:** introduce new words only when the learner's active vocabulary at that level is strong enough to keep quiz sentences at ~90–95% known. This supports `confidence_gate` and `R_FLOOR` in P(new).

#### Sustainable new-word rate (SRS practice)

| Source | Recommendation |
|--------|----------------|
| Anki / FSRS community | 10–20 new cards/day; desired retention 0.90 |
| StudyThai / general SLA | 5–10/day beginners; 10–15 month 1–3; review load compounds fast |
| Rule of thumb | Each new word → ~5+ future reviews over 30 days |

With Lingraft's N=10 quiz (**S=7, K=3**), **1–2 new words per session** is sustainable if the user completes one quiz daily — aligns with research.

#### A0 is non-standard

Official CEFR starts at A1. **A0** is a pre-A1 band used by apps for absolute beginners (survival phrases, top-frequency words). No research consensus on A0 size; **100–300 words** is a reasonable app-level milestone before formal A1 content.

