## New-item probability (vocab + grammar)

Core principle: **strengthen before expand**. P(new) gates whether new items enter the candidate pool; the **priority queue** decides if they win slots against due/weak items.

### P(new) — gates new items entering the pool (both kinds)

Same formula, kind-specific constants and backlog thresholds (see table above).

```
P(new) = 0                         if due_backlog_kind > BACKLOG_WARN_kind
       = headroom × confidence_gate otherwise
```

Grammar: pool always includes **all** curriculum topics at level L. New L+1 topics sprinkled only when vocab `level_progress` allows; weighted into pool at low priority. **No hard block** on weak topics — low R already increases selection weight via `(1-R)`.

| Situation | Behavior |
|-----------|----------|
| New user, < MIN_DATA touched | Static: 2 weak-priority A0 words + max 2 new |
| avg_R < R_FLOOR | No new in pool; MIN_WEAK_SLOTS filled |
| Healthy A0, 1 grammar topic very weak | That topic may fill 2+ of K=3 slots via priority |
| Good at all A0 grammar (high avg R) | All 10 topics still in pool; higher-R topics selected less often but never zero |
| Grammar avg_R low | Low-R topics dominate K=3 via `(1-R)` weights; all topics remain eligible |

